# XSH send()
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# Sockets are local: socketpair() and AF_UNIX sockets in the test's own
# directory, and loopback AF_INET with ephemeral ports. Every blocking call
# is guarded by an alarm (UNRESOLVED if it fires) or made non-blocking. A
# failed call may have been partly performed, so tests do not look at a
# buffer after a failure except where the page says no data is transmitted.
# Not tested: MSG_OOB and MSG_EOR (protocol-specific), EOPNOTSUPP (no flag
# value is known to be unsupported), ECONNRESET (cannot be arranged
# reliably), EINTR, and the may-fail errors that depend on the network.

assertion  XSH.send.001
anchor     functions/send.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/send.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a connected stream socket returns the number of bytes
           given, and two calls (5 bytes, then 7) arrive at the peer as
           those 12 bytes in order.
test       t/001-stream-data.c

assertion  XSH.send.002
anchor     functions/send.html#DESCRIPTION
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a connectionless-mode (datagram) socket that has a
           pre-specified peer sends the message to that peer: the whole
           message, with the stated length as the return value, arrives.
test       t/002-dgram-peer.c

assertion  XSH.send.003
anchor     functions/send.html#APPLICATION USAGE
class      required
since      2001
says       With flags 0, send() on a socket behaves as write() does: the
           bytes arrive at the peer and read() on the peer returns them.
test       t/003-read-equivalence.c

assertion  XSH.send.004
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/ENOTCONN
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a stream socket that was never connected fails with
           ENOTCONN.
test       t/004-enotconn.c

assertion  XSH.send.005
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EDESTADDRREQ
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/ENOTCONN
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a datagram socket that is neither bound nor connected
           fails, with EDESTADDRREQ or ENOTCONN (both entries describe the
           case, XSH 2.3).
note       See SI-96: three of the entries overlap here.
test       t/005-edestaddrreq.c

assertion  XSH.send.006
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EBADF
class      required
since      2001
says       send() with a descriptor that is not open fails with EBADF (-1,
           and a descriptor that has been closed).
test       t/006-ebadf.c

assertion  XSH.send.007
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/ENOTSOCK
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a descriptor that is not a socket (a pipe end, a
           regular file) fails with ENOTSOCK.
test       t/007-enotsock.c

assertion  XSH.send.008
anchor     functions/send.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EPIPE
anchor     functions/V2_chap02.html#2.10.14
class      required
since      2008
says       send() with MSG_NOSIGNAL on a stream socket whose peer has gone
           fails (EPIPE, or ECONNRESET or ENOTCONN if the system reports
           the case that way) and no SIGPIPE is generated.
note       Q-61 allows the three errors for a peer that has gone; the
           assertion is the signal suppression, which XSH 2.10.14 states
           for the send operation whichever error is reported.
test       t/008-nosignal.c

assertion  XSH.send.009
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EPIPE
class      disputed
since      2001
says       send() without MSG_NOSIGNAL on a stream socket whose peer has
           closed fails with EPIPE and SIGPIPE is generated for the
           calling thread.
note       disputed (Q-61): the same question as for write(): a peer that
           is gone may be reported as ECONNRESET or ENOTCONN, without
           SIGPIPE. The test takes the reading that EPIPE with SIGPIPE is
           the result for a socket no longer connected; a FAIL is logged
           as INSPECT. SIGPIPE is checked as pending while blocked.
test       t/009-sigpipe.c

assertion  XSH.send.010
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EPIPE
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a connected stream socket whose own side has been shut
           down for writing fails with EPIPE (MSG_NOSIGNAL given so that no
           signal ends the test).
test       t/010-epipe-shutwr.c

assertion  XSH.send.011
anchor     functions/send.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EAGAIN
class      required
since      2001
says       send() on a non-blocking stream socket whose send space is full
           fails with EAGAIN or EWOULDBLOCK; each earlier call that
           succeeded returned a count from 1 up to the length given.
test       t/011-eagain.c

assertion  XSH.send.012
anchor     functions/send.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     functions/send.html#ERRORS/EMSGSIZE
class      required
since      2001
says       send() of 100000 bytes on a connected UDP socket (larger than
           any UDP datagram can be) fails with EMSGSIZE and nothing reaches
           the receiver.
note       The limit used is that of the protocol the page names for
           datagrams over IPv4 (XSH 2.10.18 gives UDP as the default for
           that socket type); the page leaves AF_UNIX limits open, so no
           local-socket size is used.
test       t/012-emsgsize-udp.c

assertion  XSH.send.013
anchor     functions/send.html#RETURN VALUE
class      required
since      2001
says       send() of zero bytes on a connected stream socket returns 0 and
           leaves the connection as it was: a later byte arrives as the
           first byte, and the peer sees no end of file.
test       t/013-zero-length-stream.c
